1767.docs:
Rebuilds the UI from any thread, when background work has something to show.

Get one with [`BuildCx::ui_waker`](crate::BuildCx::ui_waker) and move it to
the thread. Send the result through a channel and call [`UiWaker::wake`]; the
next build runs on the UI thread and reads the channel. Between wakes the app
stays asleep, so there is nothing to poll.

```
use std::sync::mpsc::channel;
use ravenrs_widgets::{BuildCx, NodeId, View};

struct Pump;

impl View for Pump {
    fn build(&self, cx: &mut BuildCx<'_>) -> NodeId {
        let waker = cx.ui_waker();
        let (send, _receive) = channel();
        std::thread::spawn(move || {
            let _ = send.send(21 * 2);
            waker.wake();
        });
        cx.empty()
    }
}
```

The web build has no other threads to wake it from; there, change a
[`State`](ravenrs_reactive::State) as usual.

1478.docs:
How a node appears to screen readers: its [`Role`], name and state.

Built-in components set this themselves. Custom components call
[`BuildCx::semantics`] on the node they return:

```
use ravenrs_core::Role;
use ravenrs_widgets::Semantics;

let s = Semantics::new(Role::Switch).name("Wi-Fi").toggled(true);
assert_eq!(s.role(), Role::Switch);
```

Without semantics, a focusable node is announced as a button named by the
text inside it, text is announced as a label, and other boxes are
transparent: their children belong to the nearest described ancestor.

2859.docs:
A repeating schedule, starting from a date given when asking for its days.
A date past the end of a shorter month falls on that month's last day and
returns to its own day after: the 31st is February 28, then March 31.

```
use ravenrs_widgets::{Date, Frequency, RecurrenceEnd, RecurrenceRule};

let start = Date::new(2026, 1, 31).unwrap();
let rule = RecurrenceRule::new(Frequency::Monthly).end(RecurrenceEnd::After(3));
let days = rule.occurrences(start, start, start.add_months(12));
assert_eq!(days, [start, Date::new(2026, 2, 28).unwrap(), Date::new(2026, 3, 31).unwrap()]);

// It can be stored as text, in the format of iCalendar.
assert_eq!(rule.to_text(), "FREQ=MONTHLY;COUNT=3");
assert_eq!(RecurrenceRule::parse(&rule.to_text()), Some(rule));
```

